# About this wiki

The whole wiki is one plain text file. Each page starts with a title, and links between pages use square brackets. I try to keep each page about one thing and link generously, so I can find anything in a couple of hops from [Home].

Files live in the wiki's attachments directory, `example_attachments`, beside the wiki, and are linked like pages, with a leading slash. For example, the photo from the Louvre on the [Paris 2024] trip is [/photos/paris/mona_lisa.jpg]. A link to a directory, like the 2025 tax documents in [Taxes], covers every file inside it.

Conventions:

- Recipes list ingredients first, then steps.
- Book notes start with a one-line summary.
- Anything unsorted goes in [Ideas] until it earns its own page.

# Paris 2024

Six days in May with [Priya].

Highlights:

- The Louvre, early on a weekday. The crowd around the Mona Lisa was unreal; my photo of it is [/photos/paris/mona_lisa.jpg].
- The Musée d'Orsay, which I liked more than the Louvre.
- A cooking class on French sauces, which is where [Emulsions] finally made sense to me.
- Walking along the Seine every evening.

Lessons:

- Book museum tickets ahead with timed entry.
- Bakeries close on different days, so check before walking across town.
- Pack shoes that are already broken in. See [Packing list].

# Emulsions

An emulsion is a stable mix of two liquids that don't normally combine, like oil and water. An emulsifier coats the droplets so they don't separate.

Common emulsifiers:

- Mustard, in [House vinaigrette]
- Egg yolk, in mayonnaise and hollandaise
- Cheese and starchy water, in cacio e pepe and in [Mushroom risotto]

Adding the oil slowly, or shaking hard, makes smaller droplets and a thicker, more stable result.

