# Grandma June

My grandmother, now in her late eighties, still baking every week.

- She taught me to cook, starting with [Grandma June's apple pie].
- She gave me my [Dutch oven] and her copy of [Pride and Prejudice].
- She knows more about tomatoes than anyone, and her advice for the [Balcony garden] is always right.

I call her every Sunday afternoon. Her favorite gifts are listed in [Gift ideas].

# Dutch oven

A heavy enameled cast-iron pot with a tight lid. Essential for [Braising] and for baking [Sourdough bread].

- Avoid metal utensils on the enamel.
- Brown stuck-on bits come off after soaking with a little baking soda.

Mine was a housewarming gift from [Grandma June].

# Grandma June's apple pie

The first thing [Grandma June] taught me to bake. Her card says "bake until it smells done," so these are my measurements.

Crust (double):

- 2 1/2 cups flour
- 1 teaspoon [Kosher salt] and 1 tablespoon sugar
- 1 cup cold butter, cubed
- 6 to 8 tablespoons ice water

Filling:

- 6 to 7 apples, half tart and half sweet, sliced
- 3/4 cup sugar
- 2 tablespoons flour
- 1 teaspoon cinnamon and a pinch of nutmeg
- A squeeze of lemon

Steps:

1. Cut the butter into the flour until it's in pea-sized pieces, then add water until it just holds together.
2. Divide, flatten into disks, and chill for an hour.
3. Toss the apples with the sugar, flour, spices, and lemon.
4. Roll out the bottom crust, fill, cover with the top crust, crimp, and cut vents.
5. Bake at 425°F for 20 minutes, then at 375°F for 40 to 50 minutes, until the filling bubbles through the vents.
6. Cool for at least three hours so the filling sets.

She always makes one for [Birthdays] in the family.

# Kitchen equipment

The few tools I use constantly:

- [Dutch oven]
- [Cast-iron skillet]
- [Chef's knife]
- [Kitchen scale]

Everything else is optional. A microplane, a fish spatula, and a sheet pan come close.

# Cast-iron skillet

Cheap, nearly indestructible, and the best pan for [Searing].

- Wash it with a little soap and dry it on the stove.
- Wipe on a thin layer of oil after each wash.
- Avoid simmering acidic sauces for a long time, since they strip the seasoning. [Shakshuka] is quick enough to be fine.

# Chef's knife

An 8-inch chef's knife does almost every job. See [Knife skills].

Sharpening:

- Hone it on a steel every few uses to straighten the edge.
- Sharpen it on a whetstone every few months, holding a steady angle of about 15 degrees.
- Never put it in the dishwasher.

# Kitchen scale

A digital scale that measures in grams. Baking became reliable the day I started using one.

Used for [Sourdough bread], [Sourdough starter], and [Chocolate chip cookies].

# Meal planning

Sunday afternoon routine:

1. Check what's in the fridge that needs using up.
2. Pick three weeknight dinners from [Recipes] and one weekend project.
3. Write a grocery list grouped by store section.
4. Cook a pot of [Rice] and wash greens for the week.

Leftover rice becomes [Weeknight fried rice] by Thursday. If [Marcus] is visiting, I plan for [Braised short ribs].

# Braised short ribs

The best thing I make for guests. It takes all afternoon but almost none of it is active.

Ingredients:

- 4 pounds bone-in short ribs
- [Kosher salt] and black pepper
- 2 onions, 2 carrots, and 2 celery stalks, chopped
- 6 cloves [Garlic], smashed
- 2 tablespoons tomato paste
- 1 bottle dry red wine
- 2 cups beef stock (see [Making stock])
- Thyme and bay leaves

Steps:

1. Salt the ribs the night before if possible.
2. Sear the ribs in batches in a [Dutch oven] until deeply browned. See [Searing].
3. Soften the vegetables in the fat, then add the garlic and tomato paste.
4. Add the wine, scrape up the browned bits, and reduce by half.
5. Return the ribs, add stock to come two-thirds up the sides, and add the herbs.
6. Cover and braise at 300°F for about three hours, until a fork slides in easily. See [Braising].
7. Skim the fat, reduce the liquid to a sauce, and serve over polenta or mashed potatoes.

It's better the next day. Chilling it overnight also makes the fat easy to lift off.

# Rice

I keep three kinds:

- Short-grain white rice for everyday meals and [Weeknight fried rice]
- Arborio for [Mushroom risotto]
- Jasmine for anything with curry

Rinse short-grain and jasmine rice until the water runs clear. Don't rinse arborio, since its surface starch is what makes risotto creamy.

# Pantry

Ingredients I keep stocked, because almost every recipe uses one of them:

- [Garlic]
- [Miso]
- [Olive oil]
- [Kosher salt]
- [Parmesan]
- [Rice]

Also always on hand: canned tomatoes, onions, lemons, soy sauce, eggs, butter, flour, and dried pasta.

# Garlic

Used in nearly everything. Raw garlic is sharp; cooked slowly it turns sweet and mellow.

- Smash a clove with the flat of the [Chef's knife] to peel it easily.
- Add it after the onions, since it burns faster.
- Pre-peeled garlic in a jar tastes flat. Buy whole heads.

Appears in [Braised short ribs], [Shakshuka], [Basil pesto], [Napa cabbage kimchi], and [Weeknight fried rice].

# Kosher salt

Diamond Crystal, specifically. Its flakes are lighter than other brands, so recipes that say "kosher salt" can mean very different amounts by volume. Weigh it if a recipe gives grams.

- Salt meat ahead of time, ideally the night before, for better seasoning and [Searing].
- Salt pasta water so it tastes like mild seawater.

# Olive oil

I keep two: an inexpensive one for cooking and a good one for finishing.

- Use the good one in [House vinaigrette] and [Basil pesto], or drizzled on soup.
- Store it away from heat and light, and use it within a few months of opening.

# Parmesan

Real Parmigiano-Reggiano, bought by the wedge.

- Save the rinds in the freezer and add one to soups and [Making stock].
- Grate it finely with a microplane so it melts into [Mushroom risotto] and [Basil pesto].

