Measure the actual plywood thickness before cutting the dadoes. See [Bookshelf build].

# Fermentation

Letting bacteria and yeast transform food. It preserves food, adds sourness and depth, and is mostly a matter of controlling salt, temperature, and time.

What I ferment:

- [Sourdough starter] and [Sourdough bread]: wild yeast and lactic acid bacteria
- [Napa cabbage kimchi]: lactic acid bacteria in a salty environment
- [Miso] is fermented too, though I buy it

Warmer temperatures speed fermentation up; cooler ones slow it down and often make the flavor more complex.

# Miso

Fermented soybean paste. It adds savory depth to far more than [Miso soup].

- White miso is milder and sweeter; red miso is saltier and stronger.
- Whisk it into butter for vegetables, or into dressings in place of salt.
- It keeps for a year in the fridge.

See [Fermentation].

